WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.200
Inside the cortex, why don't interneurons dive into the cortical plate at once?

2
00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.950
Picture the young cortical wall.

3
00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:15.070
Under the meninges, the brain's covering, lies the marginal zone.

4
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:17.510
Below it is the cortical plate.

5
00:00:18.483 --> 00:00:22.463
Deeper still are the intermediate zone and the subventricular zone.

6
00:00:23.483 --> 00:00:25.563
Interneurons travel in two streams.

7
00:00:26.463 --> 00:00:30.963
One runs in the marginal zone, the other deep in the subventricular and lower

8
00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:31.963
intermediate zones.

9
00:00:33.017 --> 00:00:38.157
Both lanes are lined with a chemokine, a small secreted protein first known for

10
00:00:38.157 --> 00:00:39.277
steering immune cells.

11
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.170
It is called CXCL12.

12
00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:44.330
The meninges make it above.

13
00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.330
Dividing precursors of projection neurons make it below.

14
00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:52.813
The interneurons carry its receptor, CXCR4.

15
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:59.327
In a chamber assay, precursor cells moved toward CXCL12 placed on one side.

16
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.580
Spread evenly on both sides, it gave no extra crossing of the membrane.

17
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:09.510
In this assay, extra crossing needed a difference in concentration.

18
00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.670
Now take the receptor away.

19
00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:17.950
Where do the interneurons end up?

20
00:01:24.233 --> 00:01:28.313
In 2008, two groups blocked or deleted the receptor in mice.

21
00:01:29.383 --> 00:01:33.383
The interneurons left their streams and entered the cortical plate early.

22
00:01:34.243 --> 00:01:36.443
And they spread less widely across the cortex.

23
00:01:37.533 --> 00:01:42.093
Many late-born cells that entered early ended up too deep, though not all did.

24
00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:50.347
Early entry and narrow spread can come from one defect, not two.

25
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.480
If cells spent the same time in the lane, moving more slowly would narrow the

26
00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:57.580
spread, but they would not enter early.

27
00:01:58.633 --> 00:02:02.073
If they leave the lane sooner, they spread less and enter early.

28
00:02:02.893 --> 00:02:03.913
That matches the mutants.

29
00:02:05.017 --> 00:02:06.897
Normal cells must leave too.

30
00:02:07.797 --> 00:02:11.377
Embryonic interneurons gathered near beads soaked in the chemokine.

31
00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:14.537
Interneurons from newborn mice did not.

32
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.800
So to leave a lane, a cell may stop answering the signal.

33
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.080
That compares ages, not single cells.

34
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.020
It fits the idea but does not prove it.

35
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.660
The cortical plate also makes Neuregulin-3.

36
00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.760
It attracts interneurons that carry its receptor, ErbB4.

37
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.650
How the two are coordinated is still being worked out.

38
00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.230
Then the cells turn into the plate, toward their layer.

39
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.680
Born outside the cortex, steered by what the cell can read.

40
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:48.760
In the lanes, chemokine signalling helps hold a cell in place.
